386→                        <div id="content">Let $f(z)=\sum_{0\leq k\leq n} \epsilon_k z^k$ be a random polynomial, where $\epsilon_k\in \{-1,1\}$ independently uniformly at random for $0\leq k\leq n$. <br><br>Is it true that, if $R_n$ is the number of roots of $f(z)$ in $\{ z\in \mathbb{C} : \lvert z\rvert \leq 1\}$, then\[\frac{R_n}{n/2}\to 1\]almost surely?</div>
387→                        <div id="problem_id"><a href="/522">#522</a>: <a href="#cite-Er61-522" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er61','522'); return false;">[Er61,p.252]</a></div>
